{
  "concept": "An element's position in the periodic table predicts the charge of the ion it forms: main-group metals lose electrons to reach the preceding noble-gas configuration, nonmetals gain electrons to reach the following one, and transition metals break the rule by forming multiple charges.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Title text 'Periodic Table of the Elements' centered at top",
    "Purple vertical axis label 'Period' at far left with period numbers 1-7 in purple down the left edge",
    "Blue label 'Group' at top-left with blue group numbers 1, 2 above the s-block, 3-12 above the d-block (starting at period 4 row), and 13-18 above the p-block",
    "Grid of rectangular element cells, 18 columns x 7 rows, with the characteristic periodic-table gaps (period 1 has only group 1 and group 18; periods 2-3 skip groups 3-12)",
    "Group 1 cells shaded light purple with labels Li+, Na+, K+, Rb+, Cs+, Fr+ (period-1 group-1 cell shaded pale gray with no label)",
    "Group 2 cells shaded light green with labels Be2+, Mg2+, Ca2+, Sr2+, Ba2+, Ra2+",
    "Transition-metal d-block cells shaded pale tan; labeled cells Cr3+/Cr6+, Mn2+, Fe2+/Fe3+, Co2+, Ni2+, Cu+/Cu2+, Zn2+, Ag+, Cd2+, Pt2+, Au+/Au3+, Hg2^2+/Hg2+; remaining d-block cells tan but unlabeled",
    "Group 13-14 cells mostly white/unshaded with labels Al3+ (period 3, group 13) and C4- (period 2, group 14)",
    "Group 15-16 nonmetal cells shaded pink/salmon with labels N3-, O2-, P3-, S2-, As3-, Se2-, Te2-",
    "Group 17 halogen cells shaded pale lavender-blue with labels F-, Cl-, Br-, I-, At-",
    "Group 18 noble-gas cells shaded cyan with labels He, Ne, Ar, Kr, Xe, Rn and no charge",
    "Red rectangular outline around the period-6 and period-7 group-3 cells with a red curved arrow pointing down-right to the detached f-block",
    "Detached f-block panel below the main table: two rows of 14 gray-bordered cells, top row pale green marked '*' (lanthanides), bottom row pale pink marked '**' (actinides)",
    "Info/readout panel (appears on click) showing element symbol, common ion charge, electron configuration of atom and ion, and the group-trend rule sentence",
    "Legend mapping colors to categories (element-type mode: alkali metals, alkaline earth, transition metals, nonmetals, halogens, noble gases; charge mode: +1, +2, +3, variable, -1, -2, -3, none)",
    "Column highlight overlay band used by the group slider",
    "Dashed annotation line marking the metal/nonmetal staircase boundary"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "elementClick",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "That each element's charge follows from its valence electron count — the readout ties symbol, configuration, ion, and the noble-gas it is imitating together."
    },
    {
      "id": "groupSweep",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Group number",
      "range": [1, 18, 1],
      "default": 1,
      "teaches": "That charge is a property of the column: sweeping left to right shows +1, +2, then variable, then a jump to +3/-4/-3/-2/-1 and finally zero for the noble gases.",
      "_note": "groups 3-12 highlight the d-block and the readout says 'variable charge'"
    },
    {
      "id": "colorMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Color scheme",
      "options": ["Element type", "Ion charge"],
      "default": "Element type",
      "teaches": "That element-type classification and ion-charge classification nearly coincide for main-group elements but diverge sharply in the d-block, exposing where the group rule fails."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The table as given",
      "narration": "The periodic table is colored by element family, with each cell labeled by the ion that element most commonly forms. Notice that whole columns share the same charge.",
      "state": { "colorMode": "Element type", "groupSweep": 1 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Group 1: lose one electron",
      "narration": "Highlighting group 1 shows Li+, Na+, K+, Rb+, Cs+, Fr+ — every alkali metal loses its single valence electron to reach the previous noble-gas configuration, giving a +1 charge.",
      "state": { "colorMode": "Element type", "groupSweep": 1 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Group 2: charge equals group number",
      "narration": "Sweep to group 2 and every element is +2. For main-group metals the charge is simply the group number — two valence electrons, two lost.",
      "state": { "colorMode": "Element type", "groupSweep": 2 }
    },
    {
      "title": "The d-block breaks the rule",
      "narration": "Landing on group 8, the highlighted column shows Fe2+ and Fe3+ in the same cell. Transition metals can empty d orbitals in more than one way, so position alone no longer predicts a single charge.",
      "state": { "colorMode": "Element type", "groupSweep": 8 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Nonmetals gain electrons",
      "narration": "At group 16 the labels turn negative — O2-, S2-, Se2-, Te2- — because these atoms are two groups short of the noble gases and gain two electrons instead of losing any.",
      "state": { "colorMode": "Element type", "groupSweep": 16 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Group 18: no charge at all",
      "narration": "The noble gases carry no charge label. Their valence shells are already full, which is exactly the target configuration every other ion is reaching for.",
      "state": { "colorMode": "Element type", "groupSweep": 18 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Recolor by charge",
      "narration": "Switching to charge-value coloring redraws the table as vertical stripes of +1, +2, ... -1, 0 — except the d-block, which becomes a single mottled 'variable' zone. The two classification schemes agree everywhere but there.",
      "state": { "colorMode": "Ion charge", "groupSweep": 18 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Every stable ion is an atom that has gained or lost electrons to imitate its nearest noble gas — which is why charge reads straight off the column number for main-group elements, and why the transition metals, with their nearly degenerate d electrons, are the one region where that shortcut fails.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Recreate the classic 18-column x 7-row periodic table outline with the standard gaps, drawn as rounded-corner rectangular cells with thin gray borders and a small gap between cells. Left edge: purple 'Period' label rotated vertically plus purple period numbers 1-7. Top: blue 'Group' label and blue group numbers positioned above their columns (3-12 sit lower, aligned above the period-4 row where the d-block begins). Title in bold black, centered above the table. Fill colors: group 1 light purple/lavender, group 2 pale green, d-block pale tan/cream, groups 15-16 nonmetals dusty pink, group 17 pale blue-lavender, group 18 cyan, unlabeled p-block metals white. Cell text is bold black element symbol with superscript charge (e.g. Li with superscript +, Fe2+ over Fe3+ stacked on two lines where two charges are shown). Below and right of the main table, draw the detached f-block as two 14-cell rows with heavy gray borders, top row pale green marked '*', bottom row pale pink marked '**', connected to the group-3 cells of periods 6-7 by a red rectangle outline and a red curved arrow. For interactivity: clicking a cell draws a thick dark outline on it and populates a side/below info panel; the group slider draws a translucent yellow-gold vertical band over the selected column (spanning all populated cells) with a caption naming the typical charge; the color toggle re-fills every cell using a diverging charge scale (blues for positive, reds for negative, gray for zero, hatched/mottled tan for variable) with the legend swapping accordingly."
}

